{{nihongo|'''Takanibu Kinoshita'''|木下 隆信|Kinoshita Takanibu}} was a Sanese naval officer and politician who served as the 12th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 1923 to 1925. A Navy officer whose government suppressed the growing communist movement and pursued aggressive naval expansion in the [[Shendan]], Kinoshita's administration was marked by the growing independence of the military from civilian control. His death in office on 19 May 1925 cut short a premiership that had accelerated the militarization of Sanese politics.

== Early life ==
Takanibu Kinoshita was born on 5 December 1863 in [[Okayama Prefecture|Okayama]], the son of a naval officer who had served in the fledgling Imperial Sanese Navy during the [[Saito Restoration]]. The Kinoshita family was part of the new military aristocracy that had emerged from the Restoration, and young Takanibu was expected to follow his father into naval service.

Kinoshita entered the Naval Academy at Etajima in 1880 and was commissioned as a junior officer in 1884. He served with distinction in the fleet, rising to the rank of captain by 1898 and rear admiral by 1908. His naval career was marked by a series of commands in the Shendan fleet, where he developed a deep expertise in the strategic dynamics of the region.

Unlike many of his naval contemporaries, Kinoshita was deeply interested in politics. He believed that the military had a duty to participate in government—not as a subordinate to civilian politicians, but as an equal partner in the governance of the nation. This conviction would shape his entire political career.

== Political career ==
Kinoshita retired from active naval service in 1912 and was appointed to the [[House of Officers of Okaiken|House of Officers]], the upper chamber of the Diet that represented the interests of the military and the aristocracy. He quickly became one of the most vocal members of the House, advocating for increased military spending and a more assertive foreign policy in the Shendan.

In 1918, Kinoshita was appointed to the Supreme War Council, where he played a key role in shaping the Navy's strategic doctrine. He was a leading advocate of the "Southern Advance" strategy, which held that Okaiken's future lay in expanding its influence in the Shendan region rather than confronting [[Shansi]] to the north.

Kinoshita's political views were those of a military nationalist. He believed that the party system was corrupt and ineffective, and that the military was the only institution capable of governing the nation in the national interest. He was openly contemptuous of the civilian politicians who had dominated the [[Go-Shōkō Democracy]], whom he regarded as self-interested and unpatriotic.

== Premiership (1923–1925) ==
Kinoshita was appointed Prime Minister on 21 November 1923, following the resignation of [[Korekiyo Takahashi]] after the political and economic crises of the previous year. His appointment was a deliberate choice by the genrō council: after the failure of two consecutive party governments, the establishment turned to a military figure to restore order.

=== Suppression of the communist movement ===
The Kinoshita government's most significant domestic initiative was the suppression of the growing communist movement. The Sanese Communist Party, founded in 1922, had been gaining support among industrial workers and intellectuals, and the government viewed it as a serious threat to the social order.

Kinoshita invoked the Public Security Preservation Law to authorize a sweeping crackdown on communist organizations.
[[File:Kinoshita Red Purge 1924.png|thumb|right|200px|The "Red Purge" of March 1924, in which over 1,200 suspected communists were arrested]]
In March 1924, the government arrested over 1,200 suspected communists in a single night—the so-called "Red Purge" ({{nihongo|Akahara|赤払}})—and dissolved the Sanese Communist Party. The crackdown was widely supported by the public and the press, but it was criticized by civil liberties advocates as a violation of fundamental rights.

The government also introduced the Anti-Subversive Activities Act of 1924, which expanded the police's powers to monitor and suppress political organizations deemed subversive. The act established a special branch of the Home Ministry dedicated to surveillance of left-wing groups, laying the groundwork for the thought police ({{nihongo|tokkō|特高}}) that would become a feared instrument of state repression in the 1930s.

=== Naval expansion in the Shendan ===
Kinoshita's government pursued an aggressive program of naval expansion in the Shendan region.
[[File:Kinoshita Naval Expansion 1924.png|thumb|right|200px|The Naval Expansion Act of 1924 authorized the largest peacetime naval construction program in Okaiken's history]]
The Naval Expansion Act of 1924 authorized the construction of three new cruisers and eight destroyers, as well as the establishment of a permanent naval base at Karafuto in the southern Shendan. The act represented the largest peacetime naval construction program in Okaiken's history.

The government also intensified Sanese commercial activity in the Shendan. Kinoshita authorized subsidies for fishing and trading companies operating in Shendan waters, and he dispatched naval vessels to protect Sanese commercial interests from interference by [[Illypnian]] colonial authorities. These actions increased tensions with Volisania and Breisland, which viewed Sanese naval expansion as a threat to their colonial interests.

=== Growing military independence ===
The most significant feature of the Kinoshita government was the growing independence of the military from civilian control. Kinoshita, as a military prime minister, made no effort to assert civilian authority over the armed forces. Instead, he actively supported the military's constitutional prerogatives, arguing that the [[Saito Constitution]]'s provisions on independent command ({{nihongo|tōsuiken|統帥権}}) placed military operations beyond the Diet's authority.

Under Kinoshita, the Army and Navy began to operate as virtually autonomous institutions. Military officers routinely bypassed the prime minister and the cabinet to communicate directly with the Emperor through the House of Officers. The military's budget requests were submitted to the Diet as fait accompli, with no civilian review or oversight. The principle of civilian control, which had been defended—however imperfectly—by Mihashi and Takahashi, was effectively abandoned.

Kinoshita died in office on 19 May 1925, of natural causes. He had been in declining health for several months, exacerbated by the stress of the premiership. He was 61 years old.

=== Domestic policy beyond the Red Purge ===
Beyond the suppression of the communist movement, the Kinoshita government pursued several significant domestic policies. The National Industrial Mobilization Act of 1924 established the framework for government-directed industrial development, creating the Industrial Mobilization Board with authority to allocate resources, coordinate production, and direct investment into strategic industries. The Act was originally intended to prepare the economy for potential wartime conditions, but it also served as a vehicle for the military's growing influence over the civilian economy.

The government also introduced the Agricultural Price Support Act of 1924, which guaranteed minimum prices for rice and other staple crops. The Act was a response to the rural depression that had been devastating the agricultural sector since the early 1920s, and it was widely popular among the rural population. However, the price supports were financed through deficit spending, which increased the national debt and drew criticism from fiscal conservatives.

On labor policy, the Kinoshita government was markedly more repressive than its predecessors. The Trade Union Restriction Act of 1924 imposed new limitations on union activity, including a ban on industry-wide unions and a requirement that all unions register with the Home Ministry. The Act was a direct reversal of the labor protections enacted under the Mihashi government and reflected Kinoshita's view that organized labor was a potential source of communist influence.

=== Foreign policy beyond the Shendan ===
The Kinoshita government's foreign policy extended beyond the Shendan naval expansion to encompass a broader strategic vision. The government deepened the Mosellan-Sanese relationship, signing the Mosellan-Sanese Naval Cooperation Agreement of 1924, which provided for the exchange of naval intelligence and the coordination of fleet movements in the western Shendan. The agreement stopped short of a formal military alliance, but it signaled Okaiken's willingness to align with Moselle against the Illypnian colonial bloc.

The government also pursued a more aggressive policy toward Kwangju, authorizing the expansion of the military advisory mission and the negotiation of economic concessions that gave Sanese companies preferential access to Kwangju's mineral resources. These actions heightened tensions with Shansi, which protested the growing Sanese influence in Kwangju through diplomatic notes and the reinforcement of its own military presence along the continental border.

The Kinoshita government's approach to the Volisanian oil question was particularly significant. The government began stockpiling petroleum reserves in anticipation of a potential embargo, and the Navy's strategic planning increasingly incorporated the assumption that access to Shendan oil resources would be essential in any future conflict with the Illypnian powers. This strategic assumption would become a central driver of Okaiken's foreign policy in the following decade.

== Legacy ==
Takanibu Kinoshita is remembered as the prime minister who accelerated the militarization of Sanese politics. His suppression of the communist movement, while popular at the time, established the legal and institutional framework for the thought police and the suppression of political dissent that would characterize the 1930s.

His naval expansion program had lasting strategic consequences, establishing the Sanese Navy as the dominant naval power in the Shendan region and setting the stage for the confrontations with [[Illypnian]] colonial powers that would follow. The Karafuto naval base, authorized during his premiership, would become a key strategic asset in the coming decades.

Most significantly, Kinoshita's government demonstrated that the military could govern directly, without even the pretense of civilian control. His administration's open embrace of military independence created a precedent that subsequent governments would find impossible to reverse. After Kinoshita, the question was no longer whether the military would obey civilian authority, but whether civilian politicians could govern at all.

